      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the number of nickels Jamie has. Let's denote the number of nickels, dimes, and quarters Jamie has as \\( n \\).\n\nFirst, we know the value of each type of coin:\n- A nickel is worth $0.05.\n- A dime is worth $0.10.\n- A quarter is worth $0.25.\n\nGiven that Jamie's jar contains the same number of each type of coin, we can express the total value of the coins in terms of \\( n \\):\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total value} = n \\times (\\text{value of nickels} + \\text{value of dimes} + \\text{value of quarters})\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the values into the equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total value} = n \\times (0.05 + 0.10 + 0.25)\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the sum inside the parentheses:\n\n\\[\n0.05 + 0.10 + 0.25 = 0.40\n\\]\n\nSo, the total value equation becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total value} = n \\times 0.40\n\\]\n\nWe are given that the total value of the coins is $13.20. Therefore, we set up the equation:\n\n\\[\nn \\times 0.40 = 13.20\n\\]\n\nTo find \\( n \\), we solve for \\( n \\) by dividing both sides of the equation by 0.40:\n\n\\[\nn = \\frac{13.20}{0.40}\n\\]\n\nPerforming the division:\n\n\\[\nn = 33\n\\]\n\nThus, Jamie has \\(\\boxed{33}\\) nickels.",
